<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <title>Epilepsy, Seizure & ASM Trials Finder</title>
  <style>
    :root {
      --bg: #24272d; --panel: #171b22; --panel-2: #10141b; --panel-3: #202632;
      --text: #eef3f8; --muted: #aab7c4; --muted-2: #7f8c99; --line: rgba(255,255,255,.12); --line-strong: rgba(255,255,255,.20);
      --accent: #b69cff; --accent-2: #90f1d3; --green: #40d17d; --red: #ff6b6b; --gray: #a8adb7;
      --shadow: 0 18px 50px rgba(0,0,0,.35); --radius: 18px; --radius-sm: 12px;
      --font: Lexend, Inter, ui-sans-serif, system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
    }
    html, body { margin: 0; padding: 0; background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); font-family: var(--font); }
    * { box-sizing: border-box; }
    #epilepsyTrialWorkflow { width: 100%; max-width: 1180px; margin: 0 auto; padding: clamp(14px, 2vw, 24px); background: radial-gradient(circle at top left, rgba(182,156,255,.17), transparent 32rem), radial-gradient(circle at 90% 10%, rgba(144,241,211,.10), transparent 28rem), var(--bg); color: var(--text); min-height: 100vh; }
    .stw-shell { background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255,255,255,.045), rgba(255,255,255,.018)); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); box-shadow: var(--shadow); overflow: hidden; }
    .stw-header { padding: clamp(18px, 3vw, 30px); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
    .stw-kicker { color: var(--accent-2); letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: .78rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 8px; }
    .stw-title { font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4vw, 3rem); line-height: 1.04; margin: 0; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -.035em; }
    .stw-subtitle { color: var(--muted); max-width: 900px; line-height: 1.55; margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: clamp(.92rem, 1.5vw, 1.02rem); }
    .stw-legend { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-top: 18px; }
    .legend-pill, .stat-pill, .tag { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--line); background: rgba(255,255,255,.045); border-radius: 999px; padding: 7px 10px; color: var(--muted); font-size: .82rem; }
    .mark { font-weight: 900; font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1; }
    .mark.positive { color: var(--green); } .mark.negative { color: var(--red); } .mark.neutral { color: var(--gray); }
    .stw-controls { padding: clamp(14px, 2.4vw, 22px); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); background: rgba(0,0,0,.13); }
    .steps { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 14px; }
    .step { background: rgba(255,255,255,.04); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 12px; min-width: 0; }
    .step-label { color: var(--muted-2); font-weight: 700; font-size: .73rem; letter-spacing: .04em; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px; }
    .topic-buttons { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; }
    button, select, input { font: inherit; }
    .topic-btn, .filter-btn, .action-btn, .view-btn { border: 1px solid var(--line); color: var(--text); background: rgba(255,255,255,.055); border-radius: 999px; padding: 8px 12px; cursor: pointer; transition: border-color .18s ease, background .18s ease, transform .12s ease; }
    .topic-btn:hover, .filter-btn:hover, .action-btn:hover, .view-btn:hover { border-color: var(--accent); background: rgba(182,156,255,.12); }
    .topic-btn.active, .filter-btn.active, .view-btn.active { border-color: rgba(182,156,255,.85); background: rgba(182,156,255,.17); color: #f4efff; }
    .topic-btn:active, .filter-btn:active, .action-btn:active, .view-btn:active { transform: scale(.98); }
    .control-select, .control-search { width: 100%; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); background: var(--panel-2); color: var(--text); padding: 10px 12px; outline: none; }
    .control-select:focus, .control-search:focus { border-color: var(--accent); box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(182,156,255,.13); }
    .search-help { color: var(--muted-2); font-size: .74rem; line-height: 1.35; margin-top: 7px; }
    mark.hit { background: rgba(144,241,211,.20); color: var(--text); padding: 0 .12em; border-radius: 4px; }
    .advanced-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; }
    .filters { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; align-items: center; }
    .filter-btn[data-filter="positive"].active { border-color: rgba(64,209,125,.9); background: rgba(64,209,125,.15); }
    .filter-btn[data-filter="negative"].active { border-color: rgba(255,107,107,.9); background: rgba(255,107,107,.14); }
    .filter-btn[data-filter="neutral"].active { border-color: rgba(168,173,183,.9); background: rgba(168,173,183,.14); }
    .actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; } .action-btn { color: var(--muted); }
    .stw-summary { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; padding: 12px clamp(14px, 2.4vw, 22px); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); background: rgba(255,255,255,.025); }
    .stat-pill strong { color: var(--text); }
    .stw-results { padding: clamp(14px, 2.4vw, 22px); }
    .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 14px; }
    details.trial-card { background: rgba(16,20,27,.88); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); overflow: hidden; box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(0,0,0,.18); }
    details.trial-card[open] { border-color: rgba(182,156,255,.36); }
    .trial-card summary { cursor: pointer; list-style: none; padding: 16px; } .trial-card summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
    .card-top { display: flex; align-items: flex-start; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px; }
    .trial-name { font-size: 1.04rem; line-height: 1.25; margin: 0; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -.015em; }
    .status-badge { flex: 0 0 auto; width: 34px; height: 34px; border-radius: 50%; display: grid; place-items: center; border: 1px solid var(--line-strong); background: rgba(255,255,255,.05); font-weight: 900; font-size: 1.25rem; }
    .status-badge.positive { color: var(--green); border-color: rgba(64,209,125,.4); background: rgba(64,209,125,.09); }
    .status-badge.negative { color: var(--red); border-color: rgba(255,107,107,.45); background: rgba(255,107,107,.08); }
    .status-badge.neutral { color: var(--gray); border-color: rgba(168,173,183,.35); background: rgba(168,173,183,.08); }
    .card-chips { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; margin: 10px 0 0; }
    .tag { padding: 5px 8px; font-size: .72rem; } .tag.subtopic { color: #eee7ff; border-color: rgba(182,156,255,.24); background: rgba(182,156,255,.08); }
    .tag.result-positive { color: #dcffe9; border-color: rgba(64,209,125,.3); background: rgba(64,209,125,.08); } .tag.result-negative { color: #ffe0e0; border-color: rgba(255,107,107,.3); background: rgba(255,107,107,.08); } .tag.result-neutral { color: #edf0f5; border-color: rgba(168,173,183,.28); background: rgba(168,173,183,.08); }
    .summary-text { color: #d6dee8; line-height: 1.45; margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: .92rem; } .expand-hint { color: var(--muted-2); font-size: .78rem; margin-top: 10px; }
    .card-body { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 14px 16px 16px; display: grid; gap: 10px; }
    .clinical-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 10px; }
    .criterion { border: 1px solid var(--line); background: rgba(255,255,255,.035); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 12px; }
    .criterion.wide { grid-column: 1 / -1; background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(182,156,255,.075), rgba(255,255,255,.032)); border-color: rgba(182,156,255,.20); }
    .criterion-title { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 7px; color: var(--accent-2); font-weight: 800; font-size: .8rem; letter-spacing: .03em; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 6px; }
    .criterion p { margin: 0; color: #d8e0ea; line-height: 1.48; font-size: .9rem; }
    .clinical-note { color: var(--muted-2); font-size: .78rem; line-height: 1.45; margin-top: 8px; }
    .table-wrap { overflow: auto; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); display: none; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; min-width: 980px; background: rgba(16,20,27,.9); }
    th, td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 10px 11px; font-size: .84rem; line-height: 1.4; }
    th { position: sticky; top: 0; background: #151a22; color: #dce9f5; z-index: 1; }
    td:first-child { font-weight: 800; color: var(--text); width: 17%; }
    td.result-cell { font-weight: 900; text-align: center; font-size: 1.15rem; width: 56px; }
    .empty { border: 1px dashed var(--line-strong); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 24px; text-align: center; color: var(--muted); background: rgba(255,255,255,.035); }
    .footer-note { color: var(--muted-2); font-size: .78rem; line-height: 1.5; padding: 0 clamp(14px, 2.4vw, 22px) 20px; }
    @media (max-width: 820px) { #epilepsyTrialWorkflow { padding: 8px; } .steps { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .advanced-row { align-items: stretch; } .actions, .filters { width: 100%; } .action-btn, .view-btn, .filter-btn { flex: 1 1 auto; text-align: center; } .trial-card summary { padding: 14px; } .clinical-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .stw-title { letter-spacing: -.02em; } }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="epilepsyTrialWorkflow">
  <div class="stw-shell">
    <header class="stw-header">
      <div class="stw-kicker">Epilepsy / seizure workflow</div>
      <h1 class="stw-title">Epilepsy & Seizure Trials Finder</h1>
      <p class="stw-subtitle">Select a topic and subtopic, then review the trial population, high-yield inclusion/exclusion criteria, clinical results/data, limitations, and practical takeaway. Built for neurology residents, epileptologists, neurohospitalists, and anyone trying not to confuse ESETT with a Scandinavian utility company.</p>
      <div class="stw-legend" aria-label="Result legend">
        <span class="legend-pill"><span class="mark positive">+</span> positive / practice-supporting</span>
        <span class="legend-pill"><span class="mark negative">−</span> negative / failed or harmful</span>
        <span class="legend-pill"><span class="mark neutral">/</span> neutral / equivocal / context-dependent</span>
      </div>
    </header>
    <section class="stw-controls" aria-label="Trial filters">
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step"><div class="step-label">1. Topic</div><div class="topic-buttons" id="topicButtons"></div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="step-label">2. Subtopic</div><select id="subtopicSelect" class="control-select" aria-label="Subtopic"></select></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="step-label">3. Search trials / criteria</div><input id="searchInput" class="control-search" type="search" placeholder="Search trial, drug, syndrome, seizure type, alias (e.g., LGS, Dravet, SE, CAE, RNS)…" aria-label="Search" /><div class="search-help">Search is now global and alias-expanded. Try generic or brand names: levetiracetam/Keppra, lacosamide/Vimpat, cenobamate/Xcopri, valproate/Depakote, lamotrigine/Lamictal, clobazam/Onfi, cannabidiol/Epidiolex, fenfluramine/Fintepla, etc.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="advanced-row">
        <div class="filters" id="resultFilters" aria-label="Result filters">
          <button class="filter-btn active" data-filter="all" type="button">All results</button>
          <button class="filter-btn" data-filter="positive" type="button"><span class="mark positive">+</span> Positive</button>
          <button class="filter-btn" data-filter="negative" type="button"><span class="mark negative">−</span> Negative</button>
          <button class="filter-btn" data-filter="neutral" type="button"><span class="mark neutral">/</span> Neutral</button>
        </div>
        <div class="actions"><button class="view-btn active" id="cardViewBtn" type="button">Cards</button><button class="view-btn" id="tableViewBtn" type="button">Table</button><button class="action-btn" id="expandBtn" type="button">Expand all</button><button class="action-btn" id="copyBtn" type="button">Copy filtered list</button><button class="action-btn" id="resetBtn" type="button">Reset</button></div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="stw-summary" id="stats" aria-live="polite"></section>
    <main class="stw-results"><div id="cards" class="cards"></div><div id="tableWrap" class="table-wrap"><table aria-label="Filtered epilepsy trials"><thead><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Included population</th><th>Key exclusions</th><th>Result summary</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody id="tableBody"></tbody></table></div></main>
    <div class="footer-note">Educational summary only. Inclusion/exclusion fields are high-yield clinical abstractions, not full protocols or prescribing guidance. Always verify primary trial protocols, labels, contraindications, pregnancy warnings, local formulary rules, and patient-specific risk before applying.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<script>
const trials = [{"topic": "Acute seizures / status epilepticus", "subtopic": "Benzodiazepine first-line therapy", "trial": "VA Cooperative CSE Trial (1998)", "included": "Adults with overt generalized convulsive status epilepticus or subtle status epilepticus after convulsions, treated emergently with IV antiseizure therapy.", "excluded": "Patients without ongoing status epilepticus, major protocol contraindications to study drugs, or cases where emergent open-label therapy could not ethically be delayed.", "summary": "Lorazepam was most effective for overt convulsive SE among the compared IV regimens; phenobarbital was also effective but less convenient.", "status": "positive", "data": "For overt generalized convulsive SE, IV lorazepam achieved seizure control more often than phenytoin and became the adult ED benchmark benzodiazepine comparator.", "limitations": "Older trial era, relatively small subgroups, drug choices/doses differ from many modern algorithms, and fosphenytoin/levetiracetam were not part of the comparison.", "takeaway": "For adult convulsive SE, rapid benzodiazepine therapy is not optional decoration; lorazepam became the classic IV first-line choice when IV access exists.", "tags": "status epilepticus SE CSE convulsive lorazepam diazepam phenytoin phenobarbital benzodiazepine adult emergency"}, {"topic": "Acute seizures / status epilepticus", "subtopic": "Benzodiazepine first-line therapy", "trial": "RAMPART (2012)", "included": "Children and adults with ongoing convulsive status epilepticus lasting more than 5 minutes in the prehospital setting, treated by paramedics before ED arrival.", "excluded": "Patients without ongoing convulsions at EMS treatment, known major contraindications to study drugs, very small children below autoinjector feasibility thresholds, or cases requiring immediate non-protocol rescue.", "summary": "IM midazolam by autoinjector was at least as effective as IV lorazepam for prehospital seizure cessation before hospital arrival.", "status": "positive", "data": "Seizure cessation before ED arrival occurred in about 73% with IM midazolam versus about 63% with IV lorazepam, with similar safety.", "limitations": "Prehospital workflow was part of the treatment effect; the result does not mean IM midazolam is intrinsically superior once reliable IV access is already present.", "takeaway": "When the patient is seizing and IV access is theater, IM midazolam is evidence-based medicine, not a compromise prize.", "tags": "RAMPART status epilepticus SE prehospital EMS midazolam lorazepam IM IV benzodiazepine convulsive seizure"}, {"topic": "Acute seizures / status epilepticus", "subtopic": "Benzodiazepine first-line therapy", "trial": "TRENdS / PECARN Lorazepam vs Diazepam (2014)", "included": "Children aged roughly 3 months to younger than 18 years with generalized convulsive status epilepticus presenting to emergency departments.", "excluded": "Neonates, nonconvulsive events, seizures already resolved, major contraindications to benzodiazepines, or prior medications making protocol dosing unsafe.", "summary": "IV lorazepam did not improve efficacy or safety compared with IV diazepam for pediatric convulsive SE.", "status": "neutral", "data": "Seizure cessation and need for assisted ventilation were similar; lorazepam caused more sedation in some analyses.", "limitations": "Pediatric ED trial with protocol deviations and post-randomization exclusions; does not compare modern IN/IM prehospital strategies.", "takeaway": "In children with IV access, lorazepam is not magic pixie dust over diazepam; getting an adequate benzodiazepine dose in promptly matters more.", "tags": "pediatric child children status epilepticus SE CSE lorazepam diazepam benzodiazepine TRENdS PECARN"}, {"topic": "Acute seizures / status epilepticus", "subtopic": "Refractory / ICU SE strategies", "trial": "HYBERNATUS (2016)", "included": "Adults with convulsive status epilepticus requiring mechanical ventilation and ICU-level care, randomized to induced hypothermia plus standard care versus standard care.", "excluded": "Children, pregnancy, major contraindications to hypothermia, severe comorbidity limiting outcome assessment, or inability to initiate protocol cooling.", "summary": "Therapeutic hypothermia did not improve 90-day functional outcome after convulsive SE despite some antiseizure surrogate signals.", "status": "negative", "data": "Good functional outcome at 90 days was not significantly increased with hypothermia; hypothermia reduced progression to EEG-confirmed SE in some analyses.", "limitations": "Only a subset had definite ongoing SE at randomization, and the intervention adds ICU complexity, infection/coagulopathy risk, and resource burden.", "takeaway": "Cooling a refractory SE patient is not a proven neuroprotection hack. Treat seizures, cause, temperature, and ICU complications like an adult.", "tags": "HYBERNATUS hypothermia refractory status epilepticus ICU convulsive SE cooling neuroprotection"}, {"topic": "Acute seizures / status epilepticus", "subtopic": "Second-line benzodiazepine-refractory SE", "trial": "ESETT (2019)", "included": "Children and adults aged 2 years or older with benzodiazepine-refractory convulsive status epilepticus requiring second-line IV therapy.", "excluded": "Age under 2 years, pregnancy in many protocol contexts, known contraindication to trial drugs, immediately life-threatening instability requiring other therapy, or nonconvulsive-only seizures.", "summary": "Levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, and valproate had similar efficacy and safety for benzodiazepine-refractory convulsive SE.", "status": "neutral", "data": "About half of patients in each arm achieved seizure cessation with improved responsiveness by 60 minutes without additional antiseizure medication.", "limitations": "Not powered to prove small subgroup differences; clinical choice still depends on pregnancy, liver disease, thrombocytopenia, arrhythmia risk, drug interactions, and local logistics.", "takeaway": "After adequate benzodiazepines, LEV, VPA, and fosphenytoin are all defensible; choose the drug that does not ambush your specific patient.", "tags": "ESETT status epilepticus refractory established benzodiazepine levetiracetam keppra fosphenytoin phenytoin valproate depakote second line"}, {"topic": "Acute seizures / status epilepticus", "subtopic": "Second-line benzodiazepine-refractory SE", "trial": "EcLiPSE (2019)", "included": "Children aged 6 months to 18 years with convulsive status epilepticus persisting after first-line benzodiazepine treatment.", "excluded": "Neonates, nonconvulsive seizures, seizures stopped before randomization, contraindication to levetiracetam or phenytoin, or need for immediate anesthesia/intubation outside protocol.", "summary": "Levetiracetam was not superior to phenytoin for pediatric second-line convulsive SE, but was a reasonable alternative.", "status": "neutral", "data": "Time to seizure cessation was not significantly better with levetiracetam; safety profiles were broadly acceptable.", "limitations": "Open-label pragmatic design and pediatric ED setting; underpowered for rare serious adverse events and not a fosphenytoin trial.", "takeaway": "LEV can be used instead of phenytoin in many kids, but the trial did not crown it king of second-line SE.", "tags": "EcLiPSE pediatric status epilepticus levetiracetam phenytoin second line benzodiazepine refractory CSE"}, {"topic": "Acute seizures / status epilepticus", "subtopic": "Second-line benzodiazepine-refractory SE", "trial": "ConSEPT (2019)", "included": "Children aged about 3 months to 16 years with convulsive status epilepticus requiring second-line therapy after benzodiazepines in EDs in Australia/New Zealand.", "excluded": "Neonates, nonconvulsive seizures, known contraindications, seizures resolved before therapy, or need for immediate non-protocol escalation.", "summary": "Levetiracetam was not superior to phenytoin for pediatric second-line convulsive SE; sequential use of both reduced failure before anesthesia.", "status": "neutral", "data": "Single-drug seizure control was roughly 50–60% for both levetiracetam and phenytoin; giving the alternate drug next often avoided immediate intubation/anesthesia.", "limitations": "Open-label and region-specific; extrapolation to fosphenytoin, valproate, and adult practice requires caution.", "takeaway": "In pediatric SE, second-line therapy is not a religion. LEV and phenytoin both work imperfectly; move fast and escalate intelligently.", "tags": "ConSEPT pediatric child status epilepticus levetiracetam phenytoin second line refractory benzodiazepine"}, {"topic": "Acute seizures / status epilepticus", "subtopic": "Post-anoxic rhythmic/periodic EEG patterns", "trial": "TELSTAR (2022)", "included": "Comatose adults after cardiac arrest with rhythmic or periodic EEG patterns concerning for ictal-interictal continuum activity, randomized to intensive antiseizure suppression versus standard care.", "excluded": "Patients awake after arrest, absent qualifying EEG patterns, unsurvivable comorbidity, major treatment limitations, or cases where aggressive care was not pursued.", "summary": "Aggressive antiseizure suppression of rhythmic/periodic EEG patterns after cardiac arrest did not improve neurologic outcome.", "status": "negative", "data": "Suppression of EEG activity was achievable, but poor neurologic outcome remained overwhelmingly common and was not improved by intensive ASM/anesthetic strategy.", "limitations": "Post-anoxic EEG patterns are biologically heterogeneous; results do not apply to otherwise viable patients with definite nonconvulsive SE from reversible causes.", "takeaway": "You can silence the EEG and still not rescue the cortex. TELSTAR is the antidote to treating every post-arrest periodic pattern like curable SE.", "tags": "TELSTAR cardiac arrest post anoxic rhythmic periodic discharges EEG nonconvulsive status epilepticus ICU coma"}, {"topic": "New diagnosis / first seizure / ASM strategy", "subtopic": "Initial monotherapy selection", "trial": "VA Cooperative Monotherapy Trial (1985/1992)", "included": "Adults with newly diagnosed or previously untreated partial/focal seizures or generalized tonic-clonic seizures randomized to classic ASMs including carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, and primidone.", "excluded": "Progressive neurologic disease, major contraindications to study drugs, nonepileptic events, or seizure types requiring different syndrome-specific therapy.", "summary": "Carbamazepine and phenytoin generally outperformed phenobarbital/primidone on combined efficacy and tolerability in focal epilepsy-era monotherapy comparisons.", "status": "positive", "data": "The trial helped establish carbamazepine and phenytoin as classic focal epilepsy standards before newer ASMs changed tolerability expectations.", "limitations": "Old drugs, old diagnostics, and outcomes focused on retention/control rather than modern quality-of-life and interaction concerns.", "takeaway": "This is historical backbone: phenobarbital works, but tolerability is why it is not the default outpatient focal epilepsy love language.", "tags": "VA cooperative monotherapy carbamazepine phenytoin phenobarbital primidone focal partial tonic clonic epilepsy"}, {"topic": "New diagnosis / first seizure / ASM strategy", "subtopic": "ASM withdrawal", "trial": "MRC Antiepileptic Drug Withdrawal Study (1991)", "included": "Children and adults with epilepsy who had been seizure-free for at least 2 years and were randomized to continue ASMs or withdraw them gradually.", "excluded": "Ongoing seizures, unstable epilepsy, inability to safely taper, or clinician concern that withdrawal risk was unacceptable.", "summary": "ASM withdrawal increased seizure recurrence risk, especially in the first 1–2 years after taper, though some patients remained seizure-free.", "status": "negative", "data": "Relapse was substantially more common after withdrawal than continuation during early follow-up; long-term prognosis varied by epilepsy type and risk factors.", "limitations": "Older drug era and broad epilepsy phenotypes; modern MRI/EEG, syndrome diagnosis, occupational/driving risks, and patient goals must refine the estimate.", "takeaway": "Stopping ASMs is reasonable in selected patients, but the relapse risk is real. The seizure goblin collects interest early after taper.", "tags": "withdrawal antiseizure medication discontinue seizure free remission recurrence MRC AED ASM taper"}, {"topic": "New diagnosis / first seizure / ASM strategy", "subtopic": "First seizure and ASM timing", "trial": "FIRST Group First-Seizure Trial (1993)", "included": "Children and adults with a first unprovoked tonic-clonic seizure or clinically significant first seizure event, randomized to immediate antiseizure medication or deferred treatment.", "excluded": "Acute symptomatic seizures, progressive neurologic disease requiring different management, prior epilepsy treatment, or uncertain seizure diagnosis.", "summary": "Immediate ASM reduced early seizure recurrence but did not clearly improve long-term remission prognosis.", "status": "neutral", "data": "Early recurrence was lower with immediate treatment, but long-term seizure remission was similar once deferred patients were treated after recurrence.", "limitations": "Older ASM era and less modern MRI/EEG risk stratification; not a trial of individualized recurrence-risk counseling.", "takeaway": "Treating after a first seizure buys short-term recurrence reduction, not a lifetime warranty. The decision belongs in the recurrence-risk/side-effect conversation.", "tags": "first seizure unprovoked seizure immediate treatment deferred antiepileptic drug ASM recurrence remission"}, {"topic": "New diagnosis / first seizure / ASM strategy", "subtopic": "First seizure and ASM timing", "trial": "MESS (2005)", "included": "Patients, including children and adults, after a single seizure or ear