rug-resistant epilepsy with less restrictiveness than classic ketogenic diet.", "status": "positive", "data": "Multiple RCTs showed higher rates of at least 50% seizure reduction versus control/usual care in children with refractory epilepsy.", "limitations": "Open-label diet trials cannot blind families; adherence and nutritional monitoring drive outcomes.", "takeaway": "Modified Atkins is keto’s less tyrannical cousin: still real therapy, still not “just eat fewer carbs, champ.”", "tags": "modified Atkins diet MAD ketogenic diet pediatric drug resistant epilepsy dietary therapy"}, {"topic": "Surgery / neuromodulation / diet", "subtopic": "Ketogenic / dietary therapy", "trial": "Adult Modified Atkins / Ketogenic Diet RCTs (2017–2020)", "included": "Adults with drug-resistant focal or generalized epilepsy randomized to modified Atkins/ketogenic dietary intervention versus usual care or delayed diet.", "excluded": "Metabolic contraindications, severe renal stones/dyslipidemia concerns, pregnancy in many protocols, inability to adhere, or unstable acute seizure crises.", "summary": "Adult dietary trials showed responder benefits in some patients but with adherence limitations and generally modest evidence quality.", "status": "neutral", "data": "Responder rates often favored dietary therapy, but small sample sizes, open-label designs, and dropouts limit certainty.", "limitations": "Adult adherence is the entire trial goblin; dietitian support and patient motivation decide whether evidence becomes treatment.", "takeaway": "Diet can help adults too, but prescribing keto without infrastructure is just handing someone a spreadsheet and a cheese stick.", "tags": "adult modified Atkins diet ketogenic diet drug resistant epilepsy dietary therapy refractory focal generalized"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "SV2A agents", "trial": "Levetiracetam Medication Evidence Family (1998–2021)", "included": "Adults and children across focal-onset, primary generalized tonic-clonic, juvenile myoclonic, newly diagnosed epilepsy, benzodiazepine-refractory convulsive SE, and neonatal seizure trials, depending on the specific study.", "excluded": "Drug-specific exclusions varied but generally included severe renal dosing issues without adjustment, major behavioral intolerance, hypersensitivity, unstable medical illness, pregnancy in many protocols, or seizure types/syndromes outside the tested indication.", "summary": "Levetiracetam has positive placebo-controlled focal/generalized adjunctive evidence, neutral comparative monotherapy data versus carbamazepine/lamotrigine/valproate, neutral second-line SE data, and negative neonatal superiority data versus phenobarbital.", "status": "positive", "data": "Adult adjunctive focal trials showed median seizure reductions around 30–40% versus single-digit placebo reductions; JME myoclonic and PGTC trials were positive, while SANAD II found levetiracetam inferior to valproate for generalized/unclassified epilepsy and not noninferior to lamotrigine for focal epilepsy.", "limitations": "This is a medication evidence family, not one protocol; LEV is easy to use but not always the best efficacy comparator, especially for generalized epilepsy versus valproate and acute neonatal seizures versus phenobarbital.", "takeaway": "LEV is the workhorse because it is fast, clean, and interaction-light; the evidence says useful everywhere, best nowhere automatically. Do not let convenience cosplay as superiority.", "tags": "levetiracetam LEV Keppra Spritam Elepsia SV2A focal partial onset generalized tonic clonic PGTC juvenile myoclonic JME myoclonic ESETT EcLiPSE ConSEPT NEOLEV2 SANAD II renal behavioral irritability psychiatric"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "SV2A agents", "trial": "Brivaracetam Medication Evidence Family (2014–2016)", "included": "Adults and adolescents in pivotal trials with drug-resistant focal-onset seizures receiving stable background ASMs, with later practice use often as adjunctive focal therapy and sometimes as a levetiracetam-adjacent SV2A option.", "excluded": "Primary generalized epilepsy as the main syndrome in focal pivotal trials, unstable ASM regimens, nonepileptic events, severe hypersensitivity, and major safety concerns; renal/hepatic issues required protocol-specific handling.", "summary": "Brivaracetam reduced focal seizure frequency versus placebo in phase III adjunctive trials and is clinically used as a higher-affinity SV2A option.", "status": "positive", "data": "Phase III trials generally showed higher responder rates and greater median focal seizure reduction versus placebo, with somnolence/dizziness and behavioral adverse effects as practical issues.", "limitations": "Mostly adjunctive focal epilepsy evidence; head-to-head evidence proving meaningful clinical superiority over levetiracetam is limited.", "takeaway": "BRV is not magical Keppra 2.0, but it is a useful SV2A alternative when LEV works pharmacologically but behaves psychiatrically like a goblin.", "tags": "brivaracetam BRV Briviact SV2A levetiracetam Keppra focal partial onset seizures adjunctive phase III behavioral psychiatric irritability"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "Sodium-channel agents", "trial": "Carbamazepine Medication Evidence Family (1985–2021)", "included": "Patients with newly diagnosed focal epilepsy or comparator arms in monotherapy trials, including older VA studies and SANAD/SANAD II-era pragmatic comparisons.", "excluded": "Absence, myoclonic, or many generalized epilepsies where sodium-channel blockers can worsen seizures; significant hyponatremia risk, hepatic concerns, HLA-B*1502 risk populations without screening, major interactions, pregnancy concerns, or hypersensitivity.", "summary": "Carbamazepine is historically effective for focal epilepsy but is limited by interactions, tolerability, teratogenicity concerns, hyponatremia, rash, and inferiority/tolerability disadvantages versus newer options in pragmatic trials.", "status": "positive", "data": "Older monotherapy trials established efficacy for focal epilepsy; SANAD I favored lamotrigine over carbamazepine for time-to-treatment failure, while CBZ remained a frequent active comparator in noninferiority trials.", "limitations": "Evidence base is old and active-comparator-heavy; modern practice often avoids CBZ when drug interactions or generalized epilepsy phenotype are concerns.", "takeaway": "CBZ works for focal seizures, but it brings an interaction briefcase and occasionally kicks generalized epilepsies in the shins.", "tags": "carbamazepine CBZ Tegretol Carbatrol Equetro sodium channel focal epilepsy partial seizures monotherapy SANAD VA hyponatremia rash HLA B1502 generalized worsening absence myoclonic enzyme inducer"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "Sodium-channel agents", "trial": "Oxcarbazepine Medication Evidence Family (2000–2004)", "included": "Adults and children with focal-onset seizures in adjunctive and monotherapy conversion studies, often with stable background ASMs or newly diagnosed focal epilepsy contexts.", "excluded": "Primary generalized epilepsies at risk for worsening, significant hyponatremia, hypersensitivity to related compounds, unstable regimens, or severe medical comorbidity.", "summary": "Oxcarbazepine demonstrated efficacy in focal epilepsy but shares sodium-channel phenotype risks and has a notable hyponatremia burden.", "status": "positive", "data": "Adjunctive and monotherapy trials showed improved seizure control versus placebo/low-dose control; tolerability is generally better than carbamazepine for interactions but sodium remains the lab goblin.", "limitations": "Not a broad-spectrum generalized epilepsy drug; evidence is less central to modern guideline arguments than SANAD/SANAD II comparator drugs.", "takeaway": "OXC is CBZ’s cleaner cousin, not a free lunch. Check sodium and know the