cies where slow titration makes lamotrigine useless as rescue therapy.", "summary": "Lamotrigine has broad focal/generalized utility, favorable comparative retention in SANAD, and major practical limits from slow titration and rash risk.", "status": "positive", "data": "SANAD I favored lamotrigine over carbamazepine for focal epilepsy treatment failure, while SANAD II supported lamotrigine over levetiracetam for focal epilepsy; valproate remained stronger for generalized/unclassified epilepsy.", "limitations": "Not an acute drug; interactions with valproate and estrogen-containing contraception matter, and rash/SJS risk is dose-escalation dependent.", "takeaway": "LTG is often the elegant long-game drug. It will not save your status epilepticus patient by next Tuesday.", "tags": "lamotrigine LTG Lamictal focal generalized tonic clonic absence Lennox Gastaut SANAD SANAD II rash Stevens Johnson SJS slow titration valproate interaction pregnancy contraception"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "Sodium-channel agents", "trial": "Lacosamide Medication Evidence Family (2007–2020)", "included": "Adults and children in focal-onset seizure adjunctive/monotherapy trials, and patients with primary generalized tonic-clonic seizures in later adjunctive generalized epilepsy trials.", "excluded": "Significant PR prolongation/heart block risk without monitoring, unstable cardiac disease, severe hepatic/renal issues without adjustment, or seizure syndromes outside tested focal/PGTC contexts.", "summary": "Lacosamide is effective for focal seizures and has positive adjunctive PGTC evidence, with practical caution for dizziness, ataxia, and PR prolongation.", "status": "positive", "data": "Adjunctive focal trials showed dose-related seizure reduction; monotherapy was noninferior to controlled-release carbamazepine, and the PGTC trial supported adjunctive use in generalized tonic-clonic seizures.", "limitations": "Not a classic broad-spectrum absence/myo