nce epilepsy, juvenile myoclonic epilepsy, PGTC seizures, focal epilepsy comparator arms, and benzodiazepine-refractory SE trials depending on study.", "excluded": "Pregnancy or women/people of childbearing potential unless no acceptable alternative, significant liver disease, mitochondrial POLG concern, thrombocytopenia, pancreatitis risk, or drug-interaction scenarios making use unsafe.", "summary": "Valproate remains highly effective for generalized epilepsies and SE but is constrained by major teratogenic and metabolic safety liabilities.", "status": "positive", "data": "SANAD I/II favored valproate for generalized/unclassified epilepsy over lamotrigine/topiramate/levetiracetam; CAE showed ethosuximide and valproate more effective than lamotrigine, with more attentional effects for valproate.", "limitations": "Efficacy is not the problem; reproductive safety, hepatic/metabolic adverse effects, thrombocytopenia, tremor, weight gain, and counseling are the problem.", "takeaway": "VPA is brutally effective for GGE. It is also the medication where “works great” is nowhere near enough thinking.", "tags": "valproate valproic acid divalproex VPA Depakote generalized epilepsy GGE IGE JME absence PGTC ESETT SANAD SANAD II CAE teratogenic pregnancy liver thrombocytopenia POLG pancreatitis weight gain tremor"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "Broad-spectrum / mixed-mechanism agents", "trial": "Topiramate Medication Evidence Family (1996–1999)", "included": "Patients with drug-resistant focal seizures, primary generalized tonic-clonic seizures, and Lennox-Gastaut syndrome in adjunctive placebo-controlled trials.", "excluded": "Severe cognitive baseline concerns in many practical contexts, nephrolithiasis/metabolic acidosis risk, glaucoma risk, pregnancy concerns, or inability to tolerate titration.", "summary": "Topiramate demonstrated efficacy across focal, PGTC, and LGS trials but is limited by cognitive, renal, metabolic, and weight effects.", "status": "positive", "data": "Adjunctive trials showed seizure-frequency reductions versus placebo in focal epilepsy, PGTC seizures, and LGS drop attacks.", "limitations": "Cognitive slowing, word-finding problems, paresthesias, kidney stones, metabolic acidosis, weight loss, and teratogenic concerns often determine whether efficacy is worth it.", "takeaway": "TPM works, but sometimes the seizure diary improves while the patient’s word-finding gets tossed into a lake.", "tags": "topiramate TPM Topamax Qudexy Trokendi focal partial generalized tonic clonic PGTC Lennox Gastaut LGS migraine weight loss cognitive slowing kidney stones metabolic acidosis pregnancy teratogenic"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "Broad-spectrum / mixed-mechanism agents", "trial": "Zonisamide Medication Evidence Family (2004–2012)", "included": "Adults with focal-onset seizures in adjunctive trials and newly diagnosed focal epilepsy in noninferiority monotherapy comparison with controlled-release carbamazepine.", "excluded": "Sulfonamide hypersensitivity concern, nephrolithiasis/metabolic acidosis risk, severe renal/hepatic disease, pregnancy concerns, or acute seizure indications requiring immediate effect.", "summary": "Zonisamide is effective for focal epilepsy and has broad-spectrum practical use, but evidence is strongest in focal trials.", "status": "positive", "data": "Adjunctive studies showed responder benefit versus placebo, and monotherapy was noninferior to controlled-release carbamazepine in newly diagnosed focal epilepsy.", "limitations": "Slow titration, cognitive/psychiatric effects, appetite/weight loss, kidney stones, oligohidrosis/heat risk, and sulfonamide issues limit use.", "takeaway": "ZNS is useful and once daily, but it quietly brings kidney-stone and heat-intolerance paperwork.", "tags": "zonisamide ZNS Zonegran Zonisade focal partial onset monotherapy adjunctive broad spectrum kidney stones metabolic acidosis sulfonamide weight loss cognitive oligohidrosis heat"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "Broad-spectrum / mixed-mechanism agents", "trial": "Perampanel Medication Evidence Family (2012–2015)", "included": "Adolescents and adults with drug-resistant focal-onset seizures in adjunctive phase III trials and patients with idiopathic/genetic generalized epilepsy with PGTC seizures in adjunctive trials.", "excluded": "Unstable psychiatric/behavioral risk, unsafe fall risk without mitigation, pregnancy in many protocols, unstable ASM regimens, or seizure syndromes outside tested focal/PGTC populations.", "summary": "Perampanel reduced focal and PGTC seizure frequency versus placebo but is limited by psychiatric and behavioral adverse effects.", "status": "positive", "data": "Trials 304/305/306 showed focal seizure benefit; the PGTC trial in idiopathic generalized epilepsy showed reduced generalized tonic-clonic seizure frequency and higher responder rates.", "limitations": "Boxed-warning-type behavioral concerns, dizziness, gait instability, falls, and long half-life make patient selection and counseling critical.", "takeaway": "PER can work well, but prescribe it like you remember humans have moods, families, and stairs.", "tags": "perampanel PER Fycompa AMPA focal partial onset seizures PGTC idiopathic generalized epilepsy genetic generalized epilepsy behavioral aggression psychiatric boxed warning dizziness falls"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "GABAergic / rescue / sedative ASMs", "trial": "Phenobarbital Medication Evidence Family (1998–2020)", "included": "Adults with status epilepticus in older emergent therapy comparisons and neonates with electrographic/clinical seizures in neonatal trials.", "excluded": "Respiratory failure without airway support, severe sedation risk, porphyria, major hepatic dysfunction, or outpatient contexts where cognitive/sedative toxicity is unacceptable.", "summary": "Phenobarbital remains highly effective for neonatal seizures and a valid SE agent, but sedation and long-term neurodevelopment/cognitive concerns limit enthusiasm.", "status": "positive", "data": "VA CSE supported phenobarbital as effective for overt convulsive SE, and NEOLEV2 favored phenobarbital over levetiracetam for acute neonatal seizure freedom.", "limitations": "Old drug, sparse modern comparative adult data, respiratory depression, hypotension, cognitive toxicity, enzyme induction, and neonatal neurodevelopment uncertainty.", "takeaway": "PB is old, ugly, and effective. In neonatal seizures, the ugly old drug still beat the fashionable one.", "tags": "phenobarbital PB barbiturate neonatal seizures NEOLEV2 Painter status epilepticus VA CSE sedation respiratory depression enzyme inducer neonatal EEG"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "GABAergic / rescue / sedative ASMs", "trial": "Phenytoin / Fosphenytoin Medication Evidence Family (1998–2019)", "included": "Adults and children with convulsive status epilepticus or focal epilepsy comparator/older monotherapy contexts; fosphenytoin used as IV prodrug for urgent loading.", "excluded": "Absence/myoclonic generalized epilepsies where worsening may occur, significant arrhythmia/heart block, severe hypotension risk, pregnancy concerns, HLA-B*1502 risk without screening, or major interaction/toxicity risk.", "summary": "Phenytoin/fosphenytoin are established second-line SE options but were not superior to levetiracetam or valproate in ESETT and have more interaction/cardiac baggage.", "status": "positive", "data": "ESETT found roughly similar 60-minute success for levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, and valproate after benzodiazepine-refractory convulsive SE; pediatric EcLiPSE/ConSEPT found levetiracetam not superior to phenytoin.", "limitations": "Narrow therapeutic index, nonlinear kinetics, cardiac monitoring, tissue injury with IV phenytoin, drug interactions, and syndrome-specific worsening are major constraints.", "takeaway": "PHT/fPHT still belong in the SE toolbox, but nobody should pretend they are clean drugs. They are a toolbox with exposed nails.", "tags": "phenytoin PHT Dilantin fosphenytoin fPHT Cerebyx status epilepticus ESETT EcLiPSE ConSEPT sodium channel cardiac arrhythmia hypotension drug interactions enzyme inducer HLA B1502"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "GABAergic / rescue / sedative ASMs", "trial": "Lorazepam Status Epilepticus Evidence Family (1998–2014)", "included": "Adults and children with generalized convulsive status epilepticus treated emergently in IV benzodiazepine trials.", "excluded": "Resolved seizures, non-epileptic events, respiratory instability without capacity for airway management, severe benzodiazepine hypersensitivity, or protocols comparing non-IV routes.", "summary": "IV lorazepam is an evidence-based first-line therapy for convulsive SE, though pediatric data did not show superiority over IV diazepam.", "status": "positive", "data": "The VA CSE trial established lorazepam as highly effective in adults; the pediatric PECARN/TRENdS trial found similar efficacy and safety versus diazepam.", "limitations": "Needs IV access, adequate dosing, and airway readiness; underdosing benzodiazepines is a common way to manufacture refractory SE.", "takeaway": "Ativan works. The clinical trick is giving enough soon enough, not whispering 0.5 mg into a generalized convulsion.", "tags": "lorazepam Ativan benzodiazepine status epilepticus SE CSE VA Cooperative TRENdS PECARN diazepam pediatric adult IV respiratory depression"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "GABAergic / rescue / sedative ASMs", "trial": "Midazolam Rescue / Status Evidence Family (2012–2019)", "included": "Children and adults with prehospital convulsive SE or seizure clusters needing non-IV rescue therapy, depending on RAMPART, ARTEMIS-1, and related rescue-medication studies.", "excluded": "Resolved seizures, severe respiratory compromise without airway plan, hypersensitivity, very young children below product/study thresholds, or contexts where IV therapy was immediately required by protocol.", "summary": "IM midazolam is validated for prehospital SE, and intranasal midazolam is effective for seizure clusters.", "status": "positive", "data": "RAMPART showed IM midazolam stopped seizures before ED arrival at least as well as IV lorazepam; ARTEMIS-1 supported midazolam nasal spray for seizure clusters versus placebo.", "limitations": "Route, dose, and timing matter; rescue studies are not chronic epilepsy-control studies and require caregiver education.", "takeaway": "Midazolam is what you use when the IV is imaginary and the seizure is not.", "tags": "midazolam Versed Nayzilam IM intranasal nasal buccal rescue seizure clusters status epilepticus RAMPART ARTEMIS benzodiazepine EMS prehospital"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "GABAergic / rescue / sedative ASMs", "trial": "Diazepam Rescue Evidence Family (1997–2021)", "included": "Patients with seizure clusters or prolonged/repetitive seizures treated with rectal diazepam gel, diazepam nasal spray, or diazepam buccal/rectal rescue strategies in outpatient rescue studies.", "excluded": "Respiratory compromise without rescue plan, severe benzodiazepine hypersensitivity, acute scenarios requiring immediate IV/anesthesia escalation, or inability of caregiver/patient to administer therapy safely.", "summary": "Diazepam rescue formulations reduce seizure-cluster burden and provide out-of-hospital rescue options; newer nasal products are largely supported by PK/safety and open-label effectiveness designs.", "status": "positive", "data": "Rectal diazepam randomized studies reduced seizure recurrence/cluster progression; nasal diazepam programs emphasized comparable exposure, usability, and long-term safety.", "limitations": "Different formulations are not interchangeable milligram-for-milligram in practice; sedation, respiratory risk, social acceptability, and caregiver training drive usefulness.", "takeaway": "Diazepam rescue is practical epilepsy harm reduction. The best rescue med is the one available before the family calls 911 for dose number zero.", "tags": "diazepam Valium Diastat Valtoco rectal gel nasal spray seizure clusters rescue benzodiazepine acute repetitive seizures outpatient prolonged seizure"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "GABAergic / rescue / sedative ASMs", "trial": "Clobazam Medication Evidence Family (2011)", "included": "Patients with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome and drop seizures in randomized adjunctive therapy trials, generally on background ASMs.", "excluded": "Non-LGS epilepsies in pivotal trials, severe respiratory/sedative risk, benzodiazepine hypersensitivity, unsafe concomitant CNS depressants, or inability to assess drop seizures reliably.", "summary": "Clobazam reduced drop seizures in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome in dose-ranging adjunctive trials.", "status": "positive", "data": "The CONTAIN trial showed dose-related reductions in drop seizure frequency versus placebo, leading to routine LGS use.", "limitations": "Tolerance, sedation, drooling, behavioral effects, dependence/withdrawal, and CBD interaction via active metabolite matter clinically.", "takeaway": "CLB is a legitimate LGS drug, not just “a benzo.” It still behaves like a benzo when you forget the pharmacology.", "tags": "clobazam CLB Onfi Sympazan Lennox Gastaut LGS drop seizures atonic benzodiazepine cannabidiol CBD norclobazam sedation tolerance withdrawal"}, {"topic": "Medication-specific ASM evidence index", "subtopic": "GABAergic / rescue / sedative ASMs", "trial": "Clonazepam / Clorazepate Older Evidence Family (1970s–1990s)", "included": "Patients with absence, myoclonic, generalized, or focal seizures in older benzodiazepine adjunctive studies and long-term clinical series.", "excluded": "Modern pivotal-trial style criteria were